.Dd November 12, 2026
.Dt VDSO 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm vdso
.Nd the clock calls a program makes without entering the kernel
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The x86-64 kernel maps a small shared library, the vDSO, into every image
that
.Xr execve 2
starts, and gives its address as the auxiliary vector's
.Dv AT_SYSINFO_EHDR .
It has five functions, each of version
.Dv LINUX_2.6
in the library's version tables, with a weak alias of the plain name:
.Bl -tag -width "__vdso_clock_gettime"
.It Fn __vdso_clock_gettime
.Xr clock_gettime 2
of
.Dv CLOCK_REALTIME ,
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC ,
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW ,
.Dv CLOCK_BOOTTIME
and the two coarse clocks, and the system call for any other clock.
.It Fn __vdso_gettimeofday
.Xr gettimeofday 2 ,
and the system call when the time zone pointer is not null.
.It Fn __vdso_time
.Xr time 2 .
.It Fn __vdso_getcpu
.Xr getcpu 2 ,
from
.Ic rdtscp
where the processor has it, whose
.Dv IA32_TSC_AUX
the kernel sets to each processor's number, and the system call where it
does not; the node is 0.
.It Fn __vdso_clock_getres
.Xr clock_getres 2
of the same six clocks.
.El
.Pp
Each returns what the system call returns, a negated error number
included, and is found as musl and glibc find it: through the library's
.Dv DT_HASH
table and its version definitions.
Both libraries call the clock through it on x86-64; the i686 kernel maps no
vDSO and gives no
.Dv AT_SYSINFO_EHDR ,
so every clock call there is a system call.
.Ss The i386 library
An i386 program that the x86-64 kernel runs, as
.Xr ia32 7
describes, gets a 32-bit library in place of the 64-bit one, built at
boot as Linux's
.Pa linux-gate.so.1
is laid out, at
.Dv AT_SYSINFO_EHDR
with its data page below it, and the address of its
.Fn __kernel_vsyscall
as
.Dv AT_SYSINFO .
Its functions follow the i386 calling convention, and those of version
.Dv LINUX_2.6
read the same data page as the 64-bit library's:
.Fn __vdso_clock_gettime ,
with a
.Vt struct timespec
of 32-bit seconds,
.Fn __vdso_clock_gettime64 ,
with one of 64-bit seconds,
.Fn __vdso_gettimeofday ,
.Fn __vdso_time ,
.Fn __vdso_clock_getres
and
.Fn __vdso_getcpu .
Each falls back to the i386 system call as the 64-bit one does.
Those of version
.Dv LINUX_2.5
are Linux's entry points:
.Fn __kernel_vsyscall
makes the system call in EAX with
.Li sysenter
on Intel's processors,
.Li syscall
on AMD's and
.Li int $0x80
on others, as
.Xr ia32 7
describes, and
.Fn __kernel_sigreturn
and
.Fn __kernel_rt_sigreturn
make
.Xr sigreturn 2
and
.Fn rt_sigreturn ,
the return a signal frame's restorer names.
The library has no names without the prefix, as Linux's has none.
.Ss The clock from the TSC
The clock functions answer without the kernel only where it keeps its clock from
the processor's time-stamp counter.
At boot it measures the counter against the reference that keeps the
clock, the HPET or the PM timer, or the 8254 on a machine with neither,
where
.Ic cpuid
says the counter runs at one rate whatever the processor's clock does, or
.Cm rach2.tscreliable
on the command line says to take it as one that does, as QEMU's emulator
needs; never under
.Cm rach2.notsc .
Under KVM, where KVM offers its stable clock and its clock page says the
counter is one clock on every processor, the kernel takes the counter's
rate from that page instead of measuring it, as Linux's kvm-clock does,
and follows the page: a new rate is taken at the next tick, and while the
page withdraws its stable bit the clock calls enter the kernel.
As each further processor starts, it and the boot processor read the
counter in turn for two ticks, and a reading behind the other's turns the
TSC clock off.
The boot log says which clock the vDSO reads:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
vdso: clock: the tsc, 4192065 counts a tick
vdso: clock: the system call; no tsc at one rate was measured
.Ed
.Pp
The kernel keeps the clock's state in a page of its own, mapped read-only
one page below the library: the counter's value at the last tick, and for
the monotonic clock, real time and
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW
the time then and the nanoseconds a count adds, which it changes at each
tick under a sequence count that a reader reads before and after.
The monotonic clock and real time run at the measured rate as
.Xr adjtimex 2
slews it, and the raw clock at the measured rate alone.
At each tick the kernel steers each rate toward the reference by at most
one part in 2000, so that the two never drift apart and no reading is
earlier than one before it; a clock more than a tick behind is moved
forward at once, and one ahead is never moved back.
The kernel's own clock calls read the same clock while it runs, so a
reading through the vDSO and one by the system call agree.
.Xr clock_getres 2
then gives a count of the counter, rounded up to a nanosecond, for the
four fine clocks.
.Pp
Where the clock does not run from the TSC, each clock function makes the
system call itself.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/proc/PID/maps"
.It Pa /proc/PID/maps
The data page as
.Li [vvar] ,
readable alone, and the library as
.Li [vdso] ,
readable and executable, each one page, private, one free page above the
range
.Xr mmap 2
places mappings in.
The data page can never be made writable or executable, and only the
process's own loads reach it, as on Linux.
.Xr ptrace 2
and
.Pa /proc/PID/mem ,
the process's own
.Pa /proc/self/mem
among them, answer
.Er EIO
for it;
.Xr process_vm_readv 2
and
.Xr vmsplice 2
answer
.Er EFAULT .
The library, unlike the data page, may be made writable, as on Linux:
.Xr mprotect 2
may add
.Dv PROT_WRITE ,
and a write, or one a tracer makes through
.Xr ptrace 2
or
.Pa /proc/PID/mem
to set a breakpoint, copies the page into that process alone; every other
process keeps the kernel's.
.It Pa /proc/stat
.Li vdso_tsc ,
1 where the vDSO reads the TSC and 0 where it makes the system call, and
.Li clock_calls ,
the system calls of the clock made since boot:
.Fn clock_gettime ,
.Fn clock_gettime64 ,
.Fn gettimeofday
and
.Fn time .
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Count the system calls a program's clock calls make:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ grep clock_calls /proc/stat; date; grep clock_calls /proc/stat
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr execve 2 ,
.Xr gettimeofday 2 ,
.Xr mprotect 2 ,
.Xr ptrace 2 ,
.Xr sched_setscheduler 2 ,
.Xr proc 5
.Sh BUGS
The library has no unwind tables, so a debugger cannot step out of its
functions by them.
